usly when dispensing reagents like  matrigel  temperature control is extremely  important so this right hand side video  just shows you we have this  passive cooling block that can be put  into the freezer prior to setting up the  experiment  the agent reservoir is then placed on  top of that  and we can quickly add the matrigel  solution into this reservoir  and that type of setup provides passive  cooling for a period of time  and we're also working on a passive  cooling element that wraps around the  syringe so once that matrigel has been  aspirated  it can also stay cool in the  in the syringe body of course  when we're working uh with cells  and pressure cells often times with  these 3d assays  it's extremely important to ensure that  there is  no contamination between cell lines um  that there's no carry over anything like  that  so all of our instruments work with  single use  high quality consumables we control  the production and the supply of these  so we are confident that we can  deliver them for some labs getting hold  of the pet tips is a real issue right  now  so that does not affect us and we are of  course uh  very certain of the quality of the  material that we supply  uh syringes are made from high quality  polypropylene  and the reservoirs from hdpe so that  three to minimize  any passive absorption of materials onto  the plastic  and we can use a single um  [Music]  syringe type to address the full uh  dispense volume range so unlike a  petting system  perhaps where you're used to having your  yellow tip and your blue tip or  or some systems where you have to change  the type of source plate that you can  use  that is not the case here that single  clip can really address the full  dispensed range  that is accessible to the instrument um  for very large reagent supplies if  someone is really looking at setting up  very high throughput assays  on the bottom right you can see a pumped  valve  that's a system that allows us to  increase the reagent